     aaccppiibbttnn — ACPI button

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aaccppiibbttnn** aatt aaccppii??

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aaccppiibbttnn driver handles events triggered by ACPI buttons.  Currently,
     only power button, sleep button and lid status events are supported.

     The power button event is handled according to the _m_a_c_h_d_e_p_._p_w_r_a_c_t_i_o_n
     sysctl(8).  Valid values are:

           0    Do nothing.
           1    Perform a regular system shutdown and power off the machine if
                the _h_w_._a_l_l_o_w_p_o_w_e_r_d_o_w_n sysctl is set to 1.
           2    Put the system into suspend (deep sleep) state.

     The sleep button event puts the system into suspend (deep sleep) state.

     The lid status event is handled according to the _m_a_c_h_d_e_p_._l_i_d_a_c_t_i_o_n
     sysctl.  Valid values are:

           0    Do nothing.
           1    Put the system into suspend (deep sleep) state.
           2    Put the system into hibernation.  System memory is saved to
                disk (swap space) and the machine is powered down.  For ma‐
                chines supporting the acpi(4) style hibernate functionality,
                on resume a full kernel boot will occur, followed by the read‐
                ing of the saved memory image.  The image will then be un‐
                packed and the system resumed at the point immediately after
                the hibernation request.

     The lid status is set up as a sensor and can be monitored using sysctl(8)
     or sensorsd(8).

